{"name":"Wonderland","hex":"#718a70","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wonderland","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A place full of wonder or marvels.","Synonym of headspace."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wonderland"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wonderland Trail","blocks":[{"text":"The Wonderland Trail is an approximately 93-mile (150 km) hiking trail that circumnavigates Mount Rainier in Mount Rainier National Park, Washington, United States. The trail goes over many ridges of Mount Rainier for a cumulative 22,000 feet (6,700 m) of elevation gain. The trail was built in 1915. In 1981, it was designated a National Recreation Trail. An estimated 200 to 250 people a year complete the entire trail with several thousand others doing shorter sections of it. The average time taken to complete the entire trip is 10 to 14 days."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/CowlitzDivide.JPG/330px-CowlitzDivide.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wonderland_Trail","wikidata":"Q3498361"},{"title":"Wonderland (musical)","blocks":[{"text":"Wonderland, formerly called Wonderland: Alice's New Musical Adventure or Wonderland: A New Alice, is a musical play with a book by Jack Murphy and Gregory Boyd, lyrics by Murphy, and music by Frank Wildhorn. The story, a contemporary version of the novels Alice's Adventures in Wonderland (1865) and Through the Looking-Glass (1871) by Lewis Carroll, is set in New York City and focuses on writer Alice Cornwinkle and her 10-year-old daughter Chloe."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9b/WonderlandAlice%27sNewMusicalAdventureLogo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wonderland_(musical)","wikidata":"Q8031888"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/CowlitzDivide.JPG/500px-CowlitzDivide.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/CowlitzDivide.JPG/1280px-CowlitzDivide.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/CowlitzDivide.JPG/500px-CowlitzDivide.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACowlitzDivide.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Wonderland_Trail_1921.jpg/500px-Wonderland_Trail_1921.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Wonderland_Trail_1921.jpg/1280px-Wonderland_Trail_1921.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Wonderland_Trail_1921.jpg/500px-Wonderland_Trail_1921.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A map of the Wonderland Trail, from a 1921 Rainier National Park Company publicity brochure. The map shows the location of campsites for the company's saddle and pack horse outings around the mountain. Note that the indicated route crosses directly over the Winthrop Glacier.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWonderland_Trail_1921.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Nisqually_River_Wonderland_Trail.jpg/500px-Nisqually_River_Wonderland_Trail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Nisqually_River_Wonderland_Trail.jpg/1280px-Nisqually_River_Wonderland_Trail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Nisqually_River_Wonderland_Trail.jp